# Jacobian-as-value with forward-mode automatic differentiation.
# `jacobian(expr) wrt v1, v2` computes the Jacobian matrix of the
# body with respect to the listed inputs. A list body `[f1, f2]`
# differentiates component-wise (one row per component, one column
# per `wrt` variable); a scalar body yields a single row.
# The form is parse-time sugar for a matrix literal of the shipped
# dual-number `derivative(...) wrt v` cells, so evaluation uses the
# same forward-mode engine as `derivative` — no second language, no
# new engine. Each cell is exact where the elementary functions are
# exact (here exp(x)·y at x=0, y=2).
# For f = [x·y, x + y] the Jacobian is [[y, x], [1, 1]]:
# at x=3, y=2 that is [[2, 3], [1, 1]].
emath function JacobianExample:
    inputs:
        x: Float64
        y: Float64

    outputs:
        J: Matrix[2, 2]
        J_scalar: Matrix[1, 2]
        jv: Vector[2]

    definitions:
        f1 = x * y
        f2 = x + y
        J = jacobian([f1, f2]) wrt x, y
        J_scalar = jacobian(f1) wrt x, y
        jv = J * [1.0, 2.0]

    goals:
        evaluate <J>:
            produce rust.library

    tests:
        example <eval>:
            given x = 3
            given y = 2
            expect J == [[2.0, 3.0], [1.0, 1.0]]
            expect J_scalar == [[2.0, 3.0]]
            expect jv == [8.0, 3.0]
